{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Users in DBMS"}, {"type": "position", "content": 2}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.3"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when talking about DBMS users."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Exchange information about relational database management system"}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses information a wider range of contexts about exchanging technical information on software programming and software solutions, using appropriate grammar and vocabulary at this level."}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Recognizes simple ideas on concepts on software programming, software solutions and database management."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob02re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Zero Conditional"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the <b>zero conditional</b> when you talk about a real and permanent result of a situation. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "You use the <b> zero conditional</b> to talk about facts or things that can't result or take place differently. Check the following example:</br></br> <i>If you want to make a good database, you have to be careful with the DBMS you use.</i></br></br> According to the previous statement, the cause or situation \u2018to make a good database', has the consequence or result of 'being careful with the DBMS that is used'.</br></br>   You  make <b>zero conditional</b> statements by using the present simple in both clauses (the clause of the situation and the clause of the result). Now, check the structure we follow: </br></br>  <center><b>If + present simple (situation) + comma (,) + present simple (result )</div>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "The main clause is the result of the if clause (the situation). Now, take into account that when you start the conditional with the <i>if clause</i>, you have to use a comma right before the <i>main clause</i>. However, if you start with the <i>main clause</i> and then proceed with the <i>if clause</i>, the comma is not needed."}, {"type": "example", "content": "In other words, if there is an administrator, there is order in the DBMS. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "If there is any problem with those requirements or the structure of the database, the system analyst is to blame."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Passive Modal Should"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "The modal verb <b>should</b> can also have a passive form. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<b>Should</b>, as any other modal verb, can adopt a passive form. Now, we use passives to change the intention of the sentence and to give relevance to the object of the sentence (the element that receives the action).  In this case, we use the passive voice of <b>the modal verb should</b> to either give advice or describe how a person should do something. Now, the structure that we use to write sentences using this modal in passive is: </br></br><center><b>Object + should + be + action verb in past participle form + complement. </center></b></br> For example:</br>  <i>Full access to database <b>shouldn't be allowed</b> to end users.</br>Databases <b>should be designed</b> by experts only*.  </i></br></br> *Remember that you can include the subject, the doer of the action, at the end of the sentences, using <b><i>by</b></i>."}, {"type": "example", "content": "In fact, at least three of these should be included to have a functional database. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "I guess that end users should be seen as the ones who interact with the developed application.  "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "regarding "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "relating to, concerning to"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(preposition)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Now, regarding the designers, they are the people that actually develop and set the DBMS up."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob02aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "tricky"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "uncertain; difficult to manage"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Well, that isn't tricky at all. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob02aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "administrator "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a person who manages and supports a computer system"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: For example, maintaining and managing the database should be seen as the main duty of the administrators. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob02aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "limitations "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "restrictive elements that keep something from happening or acting "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: They are the ones who determine the access limitations to other users and keep everything under control. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob02aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "provide"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to give"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Finally, the one who makes sure that all the requirements that end users provided are covered in the database is the system analyst. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob02aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "set up "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to assemble or organize something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adverb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example:  Now, regarding the designers, they are the people that actually develop and set the DBMS up. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n7le02ob02aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for information about DBMS."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Describe the different kinds of DBMS users."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write down two paragraphs mentioning which are the users' roles in DBMS. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share the paragraphs with the class and discuss about it."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for information about DBMS."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I identify the types of DBMS users."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I discuss about DBMS."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n7le02ob02re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><b>Lilly</b>: Hi Carlos! It's me again!</br>\n<b>Carlos</b>: Hi Lilly, how can I help you? </br>\n<b>Lilly</b>: Well, I'm having trouble with the database users in DBMS. I don't understand who the users and what their roles are.</br>\n<b>Carlos</b>: Well, that isn't tricky at all. If you want to make any database, you have to learn that there are four different users in its DBMS. We have the application programmers, the end users, the administrators, and the system analysts. In fact, at least three of these should be included to have a functional database. Plus, you have to know that different restrictions and rights should be applied to each one of those users.  </br>"}, {"time": "45000", "image": "enpe7n7le02ob02re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><b>Lilly</b>: Really? </br>\n<b>Carlos</b>: Yes, users really play an important part in databases as they are the ones who interact, in different ways, with them. For example, maintaining and managing the database should be seen as the main duty of the administrators. They are the ones who determine the access limitations to other users and keep everything under control. </br>\n<b>Lilly</b>: In other words, if there is an administrator, there is order in the DBMS. </br>\n<b>Carlos</b>: Correct! And, if there is order in the DBMS, there is a proper organization in the database. Now, regarding the designers, they are the people that actually develop and set the DBMS up. They keep everything organized and create the relations between entities.</br>"}, {"time": "97500", "image": "enpe7n7le02ob02re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><b>Lilly</b>: I guess that end users should be seen as the ones who interact with the developed application.  </br>\n<b>Carlos</b>: That is right. Finally, the one who makes sure that all the requirements that end users provided are covered in the database is the system analyst. If there is any problem with those requirements or the structure of the database, the system analyst is to blame.</br>"}], "coverimg": "enpe7n7le02ob02re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n7le02ob02re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can list the users in a DBMS."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about the role of users in a DBMS. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can interact with a colleague about the roles of users in a DBMS."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What do you understand by privileges? Explain yourself with an example."}, {"